  "id": "russian_chastushka",
  "name": "Chastushka (Russian topical quatrain song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "A short rhymed song form, usually a four-line stanza, that spread through Russian villages and factory settlements from the later 19th century, sung to a small stock of repeating tunes with topical, humorous, satirical or love texts that singers adapt or improvise on the spot. Singers trade verses in turn, commonly over the garmon (button accordion) or balalaika, and the genre was widely issued on Melodiya village LPs and sung by state folk choirs such as the Pyatnitsky Choir.",
  "recipe": "classic modern chastushka, ballad Poetry, Slavic bard branch, Slavic baltic domestic rhythm garmon naigryshi pskov gusli. one note per syllable, solo with instrumental accompaniment. garmon, dance rhythm strummed balalaika. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
